title: Groups
section: Orchestration
desc: Put scenarios into groups and give each group a mode, a worker limit and an order.
lede: A group is a named set of scenarios with its own rules. Use groups to say that setup runs first, checkout runs three at a time and payments runs one at a time.
---
<h2>Put scenarios in a group</h2>
<p>There are two ways. Use whichever fits, or both.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Add <code>@group:name</code> to a feature, a rule or a scenario.</li>
  <li>Give the group a <code>match</code> tag expression in the config. Every scenario that matches joins the group.</li>
</ul>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="features/checkout.feature">@group:checkout
Feature: Checkout
  Scenario: Buy an apple
    Given I am logged in
    When I buy "apple"
    Then the order is confirmed</cb>
<p>Scenarios in no group run with <code>defaultMode</code>.</p>

<h2>Describe the groups in the config</h2>
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">import { defineConfig } from '@bddr/cli';

export default defineConfig({
  features: 'features/**/*.feature',
  steps: 'steps/**/*.ts',
  workers: 4,
  groups: {
    setup:    { mode: 'serial', priority: -10 },
    checkout: { mode: 'parallel', workers: 3, dependsOn: ['setup'], match: '@checkout' },
    payments: { mode: 'serial', dependsOn: ['setup'], locks: ['payments-db'] },
    reports:  { dependsOn: ['checkout', 'payments'], onDependencyFailure: 'run' },
  },
});</cb>
<p>This is the config of <code>examples/orchestration</code>. Run <code>npx bddr plan</code> in that folder to see the schedule it produces.</p>

<h2>Group options</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>mode</code></td><td><code>'parallel'</code>: members run side by side. <code>'serial'</code>: one member at a time. <code>'exclusive'</code>: nothing else runs while a member runs.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>workers</code></td><td>The most members of this group that run at once. The run-wide <code>workers</code> still applies.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>dependsOn</code></td><td>Names of groups that must settle first. See <a href="dependencies.html">Dependencies</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>onDependencyFailure</code></td><td><code>'skip'</code> (default): members are skipped if a dependency group failed. <code>'run'</code>: members run anyway.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>priority</code></td><td>Lower numbers are scheduled earlier. See <a href="locks.html">Locks and priority</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>locks</code></td><td>Lock names held by every member. See <a href="locks.html">Locks and priority</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>match</code></td><td>A tag expression that adds scenarios to the group.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>retry</code>, <code>retryMembers</code>, <code>scenarioRetry</code></td><td>Re-run the whole group. See <a href="retries.html">Retries and flaky tests</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>Time budget for the group, for example <code>'10m'</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>See the plan before you run</h2>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr plan
$ npx bddr plan --plan-format mermaid
$ npx bddr plan --explain features/checkout.feature::buy-an-apple</cb>
<p><code>--explain</code> takes a scenario id and says why it runs when it does: which group it is in, what it waits for and which locks it holds.</p>
